---
/**
 * /plant-care/ — the product page.
 *
 * NO THIRD-PARTY SCRIPTS OF ITS OWN. Not the PayPal SDK, not a React island,
 * not a widget. The only script this page loads is the site-wide analytics tag
 * in Layout.astro, which is on every page here and is a deliberate, separate
 * decision — the point of the rule is that the page people read does not pull
 * in a payment SDK that costs 100 KB, blocks the main thread, and tracks them
 * before they have decided to buy anything. That lives on /plant-care/buy/ and
 * nowhere else.
 *
 * ── WHY THIS PAGE LOOKS THE WAY IT DOES ─────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The first version of it was an essay: eight paragraphs of argument, seven
 * full-width screenshots, and a reader who had to take every claim on trust
 * until they reached the bottom. The plant-care apps this competes with —
 * Planta, PlantIn, the rest — do the opposite. Their pages are the app in a
 * phone frame, headlines under ten words, twenty to forty words of copy per
 * section, a row of numbers, cards. You can read one in thirty seconds.
 *
 * They also lean on things this product does not have and will not fake: star
 * ratings, press logos, testimonials, download counts, and lifestyle
 * photography of somebody smiling at a monstera. There are no customers yet, so
 * there are no reviews; there is no photo library, so there are no photographs.
 *
 * What there is, is an app whose screens are the most honest imagery available,
 * and a set of real numbers. So the visual language here is: the app's own
 * interface, cropped close enough to read; the numbers it actually produces,
 * drawn; and colour-blocked bands doing the work that stock photography does on
 * everybody else's page. Every image is a real capture, and the page says so.
 *
 * `noindex` until LAUNCHED. See src/data/plantCare.ts for why.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import {
  COMPARISON,
  DEMO_URL,
  FACTS,
  FEATURES,
  LAUNCHED,
  NOT_INCLUDED,
  PATHS,
  PRICE_DISPLAY,
  PRODUCT_NAME,
  SHOTS,
  SPREAD,
  SPREAD_BASIS,
} from '../../data/plantCare';

const title = 'Plant care software you buy once and own';
const description = `Care schedules for ${FACTS.species} plants, symptom diagnosis, a light meter and a pot measurer. ${PRICE_DISPLAY} once — no subscription, no account, works offline.`;

/*
 * Structured data: what the product is and what it costs. Deliberately no
 * aggregateRating — there are no reviews yet, inventing them would poison the
 * one thing this product sells, and Google penalises it.
 */
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: PRODUCT_NAME,
  applicationCategory: 'LifestyleApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Any browser. Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, Android.',
  description,
  offers: {
    '@type': 'Offer',
    price: '29.00',
    priceCurrency: 'USD',
    availability: 'https://schema.org/InStock',
    url: `https://harvestmath.com${PATHS.marketing}`,
  },
};

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can I try it before I pay?',
    a: 'Yes, and it is the whole app rather than a tour of it: ten plants with eight months of history, every screen, all 303 species, the real schedules and the real diagnosis walkthroughs. The one thing the demo cannot do is keep anything — nothing is saved, and closing the tab forgets it. No account, no email address, nothing to install.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this really a one-time payment?',
    a: `Yes. ${PRICE_DISPLAY}, once. There is no subscription, nothing to cancel, and no account to close. Updates are free forever — you bookmark the updates page and download the newer file whenever you like.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What exactly do I get?',
    a: 'Two things, from the same build. A single HTML file you download and keep, and access to an installable version you can add to a phone home screen. They are the same app; the file is yours forever, and the installed one is more convenient on a phone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which browsers does it work in?',
    a: 'The installable version works in any modern browser. The downloadable file works everywhere too, but saving back to your own file directly needs the File System Access API — that is Chrome, Edge and other Chromium browsers. In Safari and Firefox the downloaded file saves by producing a download instead, which works, with one extra click.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where is my data?',
    a: 'The downloaded file keeps it in a file you chose, in plain readable JSON. The installed version keeps it inside that browser on that device, and can export the same file at any time. The two do not sync — they cannot, because nothing joins them without a server, and there is no server.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can it identify a plant from a photo?',
    a: 'No, and that is not on the roadmap. Doing it properly needs a paid API call every time or a vision model inside the file, and the good versions are built on years of proprietary photo data. If that is the feature you want, the subscription apps do it well.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will it remind me when the app is closed?',
    a: 'No. A local file and an installed web app cannot do background notifications reliably — on iOS it would need a push server, and a server is what this product is the absence of. The due and overdue lists work. A recurring calendar reminder to open the app works well.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a refund?',
    a: 'No refunds, because there is nothing to return — the file is on your computer the moment you pay. Everything the app does is described on this page, including what it does not do, so you can decide before rather than after.',
  },
];

const faqLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

/*
 * The number band.
 *
 * Every figure here is one this project can point at: the species catalogue is
 * counted by the build, the ASPCA rows are the rows parsed from the published
 * list, and "1" is the number of times you pay. No download counts, no user
 * numbers, no rating — those are the four numbers every competitor leads with
 * and the four this product does not have.
 */
const NUMBERS = [
  { value: String(FACTS.species), label: 'plants in the reference', sub: `${FACTS.genera} genera` },
  { value: String(FACTS.careTypes), label: 'kinds of care, scheduled', sub: 'not just watering' },
  { value: String(FACTS.aspcaRows), label: 'ASPCA rows behind the pet badges', sub: 'each verdict cites one' },
  { value: '1', label: 'payment, ever', sub: `${PRICE_DISPLAY}, then nothing` },
];

/*
 * The three features that get a picture.
 *
 * Chosen because each one is a claim a reader would be right to disbelieve, and
 * each has a screen that settles it. The crops are cut from the same real
 * captures as the gallery below — `public/plant-care/shots/detail-*.png`.
 */
const SHOWCASE = [
  {
    eyebrow: 'It shows its working',
    title: 'Every interval, with the arithmetic',
    body: 'Most apps ask you to trust a number. Press the question mark on any job and this one shows what went into it — the species figure, then what your pot size, season, light and soil did to it. Disagree, set your own, and it stops arguing.',
    image: 'detail-why.png',
    alt: 'A care schedule showing seven jobs and a panel explaining how five days was arrived at',
    wide: true,
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Pet safety, sourced',
    title: 'The verdict, and the row it came from',
    body: `Every toxicity answer names the ASPCA row it read. Where a plant is not on that list it says so, instead of letting silence read as safety.`,
    image: 'detail-pets.png',
    alt: 'A species header showing a Safe for cats badge and the ASPCA row it was read from',
    wide: false,
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'A light meter that admits what it is',
    title: 'A reading, and how much to trust it',
    body: 'Hold the phone where the plant would sit. Then ask whether a specific plant suits the spot — and if you would rather just say "bright windowsill", four buttons do that and the advice is identical.',
    image: 'detail-light.png',
    alt: 'The light meter saying a spot is about right for a Swiss Cheese Plant',
    wide: false,
  },
];

/*
 * The compact grid gets everything the showcase above does not.
 *
 * Listing all eight would mean the reader meets "every interval shows its
 * working" twice in three hundred pixels, once with the screen that proves it
 * and once as a sentence — which reads as padding and makes the page longer for
 * nothing.
 */
const SHOWCASED = ['Every interval shows its working', 'A light meter that admits what it is'];
const REST = FEATURES.filter((f) => !SHOWCASED.includes(f.title) && !f.title.includes('pet-safety'));

/* Four screens rather than seven. The showcase above already carries the detail;
   this strip is for the shape of the thing. */
const STRIP = SHOTS.filter((s) => ['today.png', 'plant.png', 'species.png', 'journal.png'].includes(s.file));

const maxDays = Math.max(...SPREAD.map((r) => r.days));
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  noindex={!LAUNCHED}
  canonical={`https://harvestmath.com${PATHS.marketing}`}
  ogImage="/plant-care/shots/today.png"
>
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} slot="head" />
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)} slot="head" />

  {
    !LAUNCHED && (
      <div class="border-b border-clay-200 bg-clay-50">
        <div class="container-wide py-3 text-sm text-clay-800">
          <strong>Not live yet.</strong> This page is not linked from anywhere on the site and is not indexed. The
          checkout does not take payments until the PayPal configuration is finished.
        </div>
      </div>
    )
  }

  <!-- ── hero ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-moss-100 bg-gradient-to-b from-moss-50 to-sand-50">
    {/*
      Two decorative leaves, drawn rather than photographed.

      Competitors use plant photography here. Buying stock for a page that sells
      honesty felt wrong, and there is no photo library — so the texture is two
      lens-of-circles leaf shapes in the brand's own moss, at low opacity. They
      are `aria-hidden` and carry no information.
    */}
    <svg class="pointer-events-none absolute -right-20 -top-24 h-[26rem] w-[26rem] text-moss-200/40" viewBox="0 0 200 200" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
      <path d="M100 8c60 0 92 40 92 92 0 0-40-8-70-30S100 8 100 8Z" fill="currentColor" />
      <path d="M100 8C40 8 8 48 8 100c0 0 40-8 70-30S100 8 100 8Z" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6" />
    </svg>

    <div class="container-wide relative grid gap-10 py-14 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_0.95fr] lg:items-center lg:py-20">
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-moss-700">HarvestMath Plant Care</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-bark-900 sm:text-5xl">
          Plant care software you buy once and own
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
          A real schedule for every plant you own, {FACTS.species} species with sourced pet-safety answers, and the
          diagnosis walkthroughs from this site — working with the wifi off.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <a href={DEMO_URL} class="btn-primary px-7 py-3 text-base">Try the demo</a>
          <a href={PATHS.buy} class="btn-secondary px-6 py-3 text-base">Buy for {PRICE_DISPLAY}</a>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-bark-500">
          The demo is the whole app with the saving switched off. No account, no email address, nothing to install.
        </p>

        <ul class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm text-bark-600">
          <li class="flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="text-moss-600">✓</span> One file, {FACTS.fileSizeKB} KB</li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="text-moss-600">✓</span> Installs on a phone</li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="text-moss-600">✓</span> Works offline</li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="text-moss-600">✓</span> Free updates, forever</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      {/*
        The app, in a device.

        A flat screenshot on a marketing page reads as a diagram; the same
        picture inside a dark bezel reads as a product. Small screens get the
        phone capture — a 900px-wide desktop screenshot scaled into a 390px
        viewport is a picture of text nobody can read, on the page most likely to
        be opened on a phone. Both are real captures.
      */}
      <div class="relative">
        <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[300px] sm:hidden">
          <div class="rounded-[2rem] border-[6px] border-bark-900 bg-bark-900 shadow-2xl">
            <img
              src="/plant-care/shots/phone-today.png"
              alt="The Today screen of HarvestMath Plant Care on a phone, listing late and due jobs"
              width="420"
              height="884"
              class="w-full rounded-[1.55rem]"
              loading="eager"
            />
          </div>
        </div>

        {/*
          The phone hangs off the corner of the desktop frame rather than
          floating over the middle of it: the wrapper below is the positioning
          context, not the whole column. It also carries a white ring, because a
          dark bezel on top of a screenshot with no gap reads as a rendering
          fault rather than a second device.
        */}
        <div class="relative hidden sm:block lg:pb-4 lg:pl-14">
          <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border-[10px] border-bark-900 bg-bark-900 shadow-2xl">
            <img
              src="/plant-care/shots/today.png"
              alt="The Today screen of HarvestMath Plant Care, listing late and due jobs across ten plants"
              width="900"
              height="726"
              class="w-full"
              loading="eager"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="absolute -bottom-2 left-0 hidden w-[96px] lg:block">
            <div class="rounded-[1.1rem] border-[5px] border-bark-900 bg-bark-900 shadow-xl ring-4 ring-sand-50">
              <img
                src="/plant-care/shots/phone-today.png"
                alt="The same screen on a phone"
                width="420"
                height="884"
                class="w-full rounded-[0.8rem]"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-bark-500 lg:pl-36">
          A real screenshot of the built app, not a mockup. Every image on this page is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── the numbers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-b border-moss-800/20 bg-moss-800">
    <div class="container-wide grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-8 py-10 lg:grid-cols-4">
      {
        NUMBERS.map((n) => (
          <div>
            <p class="font-display text-4xl font-semibold text-white sm:text-5xl">{n.value}</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-moss-100">{n.label}</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-moss-200/80">{n.sub}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── the spread ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The one chart on the page, and the page's central claim drawn rather
       than asserted: these are the app's own watering intervals for the ten
       plants in the demo. One series, one hue, value at the tip of each bar,
       and the conditions stated underneath — a number without its conditions is
       the thing this app exists to argue against. -->
  <section class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-white">
    <div class="container-wide py-14">
      <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.85fr_1.15fr] lg:items-start">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">Ten plants. Ten different answers.</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
            A subscription app reminds you to water everything on Tuesday. This one works out each plant from its
            species, its pot, the season, your zone and the light where it sits.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
            The ZZ plant and the tomato are twelve times apart. Both are in the demo, and both will show you their
            arithmetic.
          </p>
          <a href={DEMO_URL} class="btn-secondary mt-6">Open the demo</a>
        </div>

        <figure class="rounded-2xl bg-sand-50 p-5 sm:p-7">
          <figcaption class="text-sm font-medium text-bark-800">Days between waterings</figcaption>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-3">
            {
              SPREAD.map((r) => (
                <div class="grid grid-cols-[7.5rem_1fr] items-center gap-3 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr]">
                  <div class="min-w-0">
                    <p class="truncate text-sm font-medium text-bark-800">{r.plant}</p>
                    <p class="truncate text-xs text-bark-500">{r.note}</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                    <div
                      class="h-3.5 rounded-r-[4px] bg-moss-600"
                      style={`width: ${Math.max(2, (r.days / maxDays) * 100)}%`}
                      role="img"
                      aria-label={`${r.plant}: every ${r.days} days`}
                    />
                    <span class="shrink-0 text-sm tabular-nums text-bark-700">{r.days}d</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p class="mt-6 border-t border-sand-200 pt-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-bark-500">{SPREAD_BASIS}</p>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── showcase ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="what-it-does" class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-sand-50 scroll-mt-20">
    <div class="container-wide py-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">The three things worth checking</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-base text-bark-600">
        Each of these is a claim you would be right to disbelieve, so here is the screen that settles it.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 space-y-6">
        {
          SHOWCASE.filter((s) => s.wide).map((s) => (
            <div class="grid items-center gap-8 rounded-2xl bg-white p-6 shadow-card ring-1 ring-moss-100/60 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] lg:p-8">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-moss-700">{s.eyebrow}</p>
                <h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-2xl">{s.title}</h3>
                <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">{s.body}</p>
              </div>
              <img
                src={`/plant-care/shots/${s.image}`}
                alt={s.alt}
                width="866"
                class="w-full rounded-xl border border-moss-100 shadow-sm"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
          ))
        }

        <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
          {
            SHOWCASE.filter((s) => !s.wide).map((s) => (
              <div class="flex flex-col rounded-2xl bg-white p-6 shadow-card ring-1 ring-moss-100/60">
                <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-moss-700">{s.eyebrow}</p>
                <h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-xl font-semibold text-bark-900">{s.title}</h3>
                <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">{s.body}</p>
                <img
                  src={`/plant-care/shots/${s.image}`}
                  alt={s.alt}
                  width="866"
                  class="mt-5 w-full rounded-xl border border-moss-100 shadow-sm"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── everything else it does ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-white">
    <div class="container-wide py-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">And the rest of it</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-7 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
        {
          REST.map((f) => (
            <div>
              <div class="h-1 w-8 rounded bg-moss-300" />
              <h3 class="mt-4 font-display text-base font-semibold text-bark-900">{f.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">{f.short ?? f.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── the demo ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-moss-50/70">
    <div class="container-wide py-14">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr] lg:items-center">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">Try it before you pay for it</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
            The demo is this app, not a video of it and not a cut-down version. Ten plants, eight months of history,
            every screen, all {FACTS.species} species.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
            <strong class="text-bark-900">The one thing it cannot do is keep anything.</strong> Nothing is saved, and
            closing the tab forgets it. That is the only difference between the demo and the {PRICE_DISPLAY} — which is
            the honest way round, because what you are buying is a record that lasts years.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
            <a href={DEMO_URL} class="btn-primary px-6 py-3 text-base">Open the demo</a>
            <a href={PATHS.buy} class="btn-secondary px-6 py-3 text-base">Buy for {PRICE_DISPLAY}</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-moss-700">Worth doing in the first minute</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-700">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-bark-900">Press the ? on any job.</strong> It shows the arithmetic behind the
              interval.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-bark-900">Compare the ZZ plant and the tomato.</strong> Same app, same afternoon,
              twelve times apart.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-bark-900">Look up something you own in Plants A–Z.</strong> If it is toxic to cats it
              says so, and names the row.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── the strip ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-white">
    <div class="container-wide py-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">The shape of it</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-bark-600">
        Captured from the built file by the project's own screenshot script, over <code>file://</code> — which is
        exactly how you will open it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          STRIP.map((s) => (
            <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-sand-50 p-3 ring-1 ring-moss-100/60">
              <img
                src={`/plant-care/shots/${s.file}`}
                alt={s.alt}
                width="900"
                class="w-full rounded-xl border border-moss-100 bg-white shadow-sm"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <figcaption class="px-2 py-3 text-sm text-bark-600">{s.caption}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── against the subscription apps ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-sand-50">
    <div class="container-wide py-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">Against Planta, PlantIn and Plantum</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-base text-bark-600">
        Three rows go against us. They stay in, because a comparison where every row is a win is an advertisement.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-separate border-spacing-0 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-white text-sm shadow-card ring-1 ring-moss-100/60">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th class="border-b border-moss-100 px-5 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-bark-500" />
              <th class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-moss-50/60 px-5 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-moss-800">
                This app
              </th>
              <th class="border-b border-moss-100 px-5 py-4 text-left font-semibold text-bark-500">
                The subscription apps
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              COMPARISON.map((row) => (
                <tr class={row.againstUs ? 'bg-clay-50/40' : ''}>
                  <td class="border-b border-moss-50 px-5 py-3.5 font-medium text-bark-800">{row.what}</td>
                  <td
                    class={`border-b border-moss-50 px-5 py-3.5 ${row.againstUs ? 'text-bark-600' : 'bg-moss-50/60 font-medium text-moss-900'}`}
                  >
                    <span class="mr-1.5" aria-hidden="true">{row.againstUs ? '·' : '✓'}</span>
                    {row.ours}
                  </td>
                  <td class="border-b border-moss-50 px-5 py-3.5 text-bark-600">
                    <span class="mr-1.5 text-clay-600" aria-hidden="true">{row.againstUs ? '✓' : '·'}</span>
                    {row.theirs}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-bark-500">
        Subscription prices change; check theirs rather than trusting a number on our page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── what it does not do ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-b border-clay-100 bg-clay-50/50">
    <div class="container-wide py-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">What it does not do</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-base text-bark-600">
        At the same size as the features, because finding this out after paying is how a product earns a one-star
        review.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        {
          NOT_INCLUDED.map((item) => (
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-clay-200 bg-white p-6">
              <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-clay-800">{item.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">{item.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── questions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-b border-moss-100 bg-white">
    <div class="container-prose py-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900 sm:text-3xl">Questions</h2>
      {/*
        An accordion, closed by default.

        These answers are the honest backbone of the page and none of them has
        been shortened — but eight of them expanded is two thousand pixels of
        text between the reader and the price, and a reader who wanted the
        refund policy can find it in one line instead of scanning for it.
      */}
      <div class="mt-6 divide-y divide-bark-100 border-y border-bark-100">
        {
          faqs.map((f) => (
            <details class="group py-1">
              <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 py-4 text-left font-display text-base font-semibold text-bark-900 marker:content-none hover:text-moss-800">
                {f.q}
                <span
                  class="shrink-0 text-xl font-normal leading-none text-moss-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-45"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                >
                  +
                </span>
              </summary>
              <p class="pb-5 pr-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">{f.a}</p>
            </details>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── close ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="bg-moss-800">
    <div class="container-prose py-16 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-semibold text-white">{PRICE_DISPLAY}, once.</h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-xl text-moss-100">
        The file downloads as soon as the payment clears, and the installable version is on the same page. Free updates
        forever.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-col justify-center gap-3 sm:flex-row">
        <a
          href={PATHS.buy}
          class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white px-8 py-3 text-base font-semibold text-moss-800 transition hover:bg-moss-50"
        >
          Buy for {PRICE_DISPLAY}
        </a>
        <a
          href={DEMO_URL}
          class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-moss-500 px-8 py-3 text-base font-semibold text-white transition hover:bg-moss-700"
        >
          Or try the demo first
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-moss-200">No refunds — everything it does and does not do is on this page.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
